#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133579 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ea335d6-6ee6-514f-acd4-deb39d50cf81" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components in Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "7aec717f-6f52-590d-9cd0-47c34b587e1c" :question/math-academy-id "e-5551" :question/problem "If ![](images/55/55d5d5dc4af3eaf8d0bf0474fd77731242346be452c109d6bbc6fdb52033a647.svg) and ![](images/6d/6dc2b50d6bb8a7d2fcc50be918737857d065088320010a356c626e1417efcc82.svg) are orthogonal vectors, find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{v}$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =5\\cdot (-4)+(-3)\\cdot 6+k\\cdot (-6)+(-2)\\cdot 8 \\\\  & =-20-18-6k-16 \\\\  & =-6k-54.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the last line to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6k-54 & =0 \\\\ -6k & =54 \\\\ k & =-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133580 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133771 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc757fa2-12ce-5a1c-a524-8eb256842079" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Dot Products Using Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "ce2ff5a8-84bb-5d2b-8078-623203f1a3b5" :question/math-academy-id "e-5552" :question/problem "Let ![](images/ae/ae5c0c967a0ddd28c163a51aca44edc3340cb76a2f9da28704563b423c591de8.svg) and ![](images/9a/9afd33bf9d62d13ceab462ed9705c007173253b065b347c6475acbff5b8090fe.svg) be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u} \\!\\perp\\! \\mathbf{w}$. Find $(\\mathbf{u}+\\mathbf{v})\\cdot \\mathbf{w} + \\mathbf{u}\\cdot(\\mathbf{v}+ \\mathbf{w}).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $\\mathbf{u}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}=0$. Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u+v)\\cdot w+u\\cdot (v+w) & =u\\cdot w+v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+u\\cdot w \\\\  & =0+v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v+0 \\\\  & =v\\cdot w+u\\cdot v \\\\  & =(2\\cdot (-1)+(-3)\\cdot 3+4\\cdot 1) \\\\  & +((-1)\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-3)+5\\cdot 4)\\, \\\\  & =(-2-9+4)+(-2+6+20) \\\\  & =-7+24 \\\\  & =17.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133581 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e02a242-6ef9-574a-be2a-2b68b35d761c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of a Vector Orthogonal to Two Given Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "8b0122e7-3500-50cf-8505-22970428dac6" :question/math-academy-id "e-5553" :question/problem "Let ![](images/1f/1fb7970633bab3a0518872b1eb14dcfdef5c95f1b6bc01760f4cff1680350413.svg) and ![](images/a1/a1a509e6431cd3b3625d4960bdcd10106da3b60a9d7f7bc9dd11089a6b8960b2.svg) be vectors such that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$. What is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Given that $\\mathbf{u}$ is orthogonal to $\\mathbf{v}$ and $\\mathbf{w}$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{v}=0$. Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ -8\\cdot 6+a\\cdot (-15)+b\\cdot 9+(-10)\\cdot (-12) & =0 \\\\ -48-15a+9b+120 & =0 \\\\ -15a+9b & =-72 \\\\ -5a+3b & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $\\mathbf{u}\\cdot\\mathbf{w}=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ -8\\cdot 4+a\\cdot 2+b\\cdot (-5)+(-10)\\cdot (-3) & =0 \\\\ -32+2a-5b+30 & =0 \\\\ 2a-5b & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-5a+3b=-24 \\\\ 2a-5b=2.\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $2$ and the second equation by $5$, we get\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-10a+6b=-48 \\\\ 10a-25b=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, adding the above equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n-19b = -38 \\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad b = 2.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b = 2$ into the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2a-5(2) & =2 \\\\ 2a-10 & =2 \\\\ 2a & =12 \\\\ a & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b = 6 + 2 = 8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133630 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce8958e6-c578-572f-8b34-5513b6f64a51" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/id #uuid "31abdef6-68e0-5c10-b03d-9d835ea6c681" :question/math-academy-id "e-5909" :question/problem "Given the vector ![](images/8f/8f1a6f36da1dacbd3e275372e06e9ff13e7f3600517066116f422d0f8cb00b47.svg), which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $\\mathbf{u}$?\n![](images/c9/c905bc151f6d6df0bfb610152baca3dfe0ff63c4789fa5377de31aa9996da61a.svg)" :question/worked-solution "We compute the dot product between $\\mathbf{u}$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =-5+9-8+0 \\\\  & =-4.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 8+0\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =-10-6+16+0 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot x & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {x}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot (-4)+2\\cdot (-8)+0\\cdot 6 \\\\  & =-10+12-16+0 \\\\  & =-14.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the correct answer is $\\mathbf{w}$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423656 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e02a242-6ef9-574a-be2a-2b68b35d761c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of a Vector Orthogonal to Two Given Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1602525e-bf2f-5fd3-827e-a1e276978ea0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423624} {:answer/id #uuid "2a702037-a4b0-50cc-97fd-b5d6788b58a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423627} {:answer/id #uuid "314612f9-0892-5598-ae9d-b402b25a618e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423628} {:answer/id #uuid "81fefe85-5730-5d6f-833d-3cdff63a604c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423636} {:answer/id #uuid "cd646d15-fc68-5176-a821-57dae3be108e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "314612f9-0892-5598-ae9d-b402b25a618e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423628} :answer-field/id #uuid "c2f630ec-9673-54b1-aa09-9f5f12f9cc84" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423651}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cc095c64-455d-525a-98ca-451d265b041d" :question/math-academy-id "q-39293" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ a \\\\ 7 \\\\ b\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Given that $u$ is orthogonal to $v$ and $w$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot 1+a\\cdot 4+7\\cdot 0+b\\cdot (-6) & =0 \\\\ 2+4a-6b & =0 \\\\ 4a-6b & =-2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ 2\\cdot (-4)+a\\cdot 3+7\\cdot 5+b\\cdot 4 & =0 \\\\ -8+3a+35+4b & =0 \\\\ 3a+4b & =-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}4a-6b=-2 \\\\ 3a+4b=-27.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-3,$ the second equation by $4,$ and adding the resulting equations, we obtain\n\n$$\n34b=-102\\,⟹\\,b=-3.\n$$\n\nSubstituting $b=-3$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4a-6(-3) & =-2 \\\\ 4a+18 & =-2 \\\\ 4a & =-20 \\\\ a & =-5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b=-5+(-3)=-8.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423654 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ea335d6-6ee6-514f-acd4-deb39d50cf81" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components in Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0126d44d-cbd6-5e5d-8f9b-fd5306980a4d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423622} {:answer/id #uuid "7baae6cc-d820-5841-a12b-4d095c694fc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423634} {:answer/id #uuid "aa5246e7-ab9c-5a4f-a3c1-b20c8c4b31a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423641} {:answer/id #uuid "b688eeeb-5eaf-5f06-888e-e3443acf2805" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "11" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423642} {:answer/id #uuid "f3487e6d-320a-578b-a1e0-1cabee888187" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-13" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7baae6cc-d820-5841-a12b-4d095c694fc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423634} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e2b0842-7869-5406-b40d-48cd2134b49c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a297bc5c-e8a7-5968-a05c-32844e09a57a" :question/math-academy-id "q-39319" :question/problem "If $u=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ k \\\\ 2 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]$ and $v=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ are orthogonal vectors, then $k=$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =(-5)\\cdot (-3)+k\\cdot (-1)+2\\cdot (-8)+(-6)\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =15-k-16-6 \\\\  & =-k-7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-k-7 & =0 \\\\ k & =-7.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186134252 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133772 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "8e02a242-6ef9-574a-be2a-2b68b35d761c" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components of a Vector Orthogonal to Two Given Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0498426e-1d67-5743-8155-cbdfddbbe8c5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324017} {:answer/id #uuid "504cd4da-c00a-5921-ad70-345cc8d353ea" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{95}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324629} {:answer/id #uuid "6949c75f-6e74-5021-8a4b-aede25760128" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186324850} {:answer/id #uuid "a3d6dbc4-7e8a-5950-9514-d157c8d4998e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{47}{13}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325362} {:answer/id #uuid "fac59378-26e0-5a77-809f-df7d10a68baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326178}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fac59378-26e0-5a77-809f-df7d10a68baa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326178} :answer-field/id #uuid "c05dc121-603f-5664-b4aa-929f1533968e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186326567}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "69f9e8f7-7103-5d3a-9398-65c91081214b" :question/math-academy-id "q-40024" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ a \\\\ b \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 4 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w.$ What is the value of $a+b?$" :question/worked-solution "Given that $u⊥v$ and $u⊥w$, we have\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v=0 \\\\ u\\cdot w=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's consider the first equation $u\\cdot v=0.$ Calculating the dot product in terms of $a$ and $b$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & =0 \\\\ -3\\cdot 3+a\\cdot 4+b\\cdot 1-4\\cdot 1 & =0 \\\\ -9+4a+b-4 & =0 \\\\ 4a+b & =13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, for the second equation $u\\cdot w=0$, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & =0 \\\\ -3\\cdot 1+a\\cdot 1+b\\cdot (-3)-4\\cdot (-4) & =0 \\\\ -3+a-3b+16 & =0 \\\\ a-3b & =-13.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, to find the values of $a$ and $b$, we need to solve the system\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}4a+b=13 \\\\ a-3b=-13\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $3$ and adding the result to the second equation, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13a=26\\,⟹\\,a=2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $a=2$ into the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(2)+b & =13 \\\\ 8+b & =13 \\\\ b & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we have that\n\n$$\na+b=2+5=7.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423653 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce8958e6-c578-572f-8b34-5513b6f64a51" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1bfdcc5b-d056-5b31-b19a-05f23514f849" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423625} {:answer/id #uuid "3546e267-373b-5e1e-9457-1de013551d43" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423629} {:answer/id #uuid "586e0aed-67d2-521e-99a4-464bd6aafb33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$w$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423631} {:answer/id #uuid "8c854f8c-bc14-576a-8c8c-169fe6f12ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423637} {:answer/id #uuid "9fd0fd2c-9a26-5e07-8a9b-2801e60a4ff5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423639}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "586e0aed-67d2-521e-99a4-464bd6aafb33" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$w$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423631} :answer-field/id #uuid "2093f599-d31a-574d-bf27-4e818cda2660" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7ead0ce1-1494-5459-ab15-c85fe87f9dab" :question/math-academy-id "q-74086" :question/problem "Given the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$, which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $u?$\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -2\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the dot product between $u$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot (-1)+0\\cdot (-3)+(-1)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =-3+0-1+2 \\\\  & =-2.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 1+0\\cdot 3+(-1)\\cdot 1+1\\cdot (-2) \\\\  & =3+0-1-2 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot x & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {x}_{4} \\\\  & =3\\cdot 2+0\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 2+1\\cdot (-4) \\\\  & =6+0-2-4 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the correct option is $w$ and $x$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403752 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133815 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "ce8958e6-c578-572f-8b34-5513b6f64a51" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c7bba809-4721-5c7c-a48f-bfd2433737e6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403754} {:answer/id #uuid "43d9e4d1-8fd6-51da-a82d-2abfd7b01414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403755} {:answer/id #uuid "0d4b2021-aa4e-51fe-8f3b-7c38e32a9c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403756} {:answer/id #uuid "52589bc7-27ee-59f4-9207-199b2fca508f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403757} {:answer/id #uuid "d2dc313f-9b1a-5d2b-9aaf-9e6ff1625af1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $x$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403758}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "0d4b2021-aa4e-51fe-8f3b-7c38e32a9c2f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$v$ and $w$ only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403756} :answer-field/id #uuid "a31d4961-a16e-58ba-afd9-33d1a3426a24" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186403753}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "96a85d0f-f840-5482-ab7d-823e5b207944" :question/math-academy-id "q-74089" :question/problem "Given the vector $u=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 3 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]$, which of the following vectors are orthogonal to $u?$\n\n$$\nv=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,w=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,x=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$" :question/worked-solution "We compute the dot product between $u$ and each of the listed vectors:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot v & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {v}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {v}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {v}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {v}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-2)+(-1)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 0+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =-4-1+0+5 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot w & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {w}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {w}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {w}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {w}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 1+(-1)\\cdot 4+3\\cdot (-1)+5\\cdot 1 \\\\  & =2-4-3+5 \\\\  & =0.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u\\cdot x & ={u}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{1}+{u}_{2}\\cdot {x}_{2}+{u}_{3}\\cdot {x}_{3}+{u}_{4}\\cdot {x}_{4} \\\\  & =2\\cdot 2+(-1)\\cdot 0+3\\cdot (-2)+5\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =4+0-6-5 \\\\  & =-7.\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the correct option is $v$ and $w$ only."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186393390 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133770 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1ea335d6-6ee6-514f-acd4-deb39d50cf81" :knowledge-point/title "Determining Components in Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2a8896dc-4ae3-500a-96fb-6731736b5fc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=0,2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393392} {:answer/id #uuid "e07b63ac-7b5e-5690-92e9-221372ce4ba1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "No solution" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393393} {:answer/id #uuid "69a990be-5c76-515e-a485-ec717c55c7a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393394} {:answer/id #uuid "3d54c806-e269-5850-af32-62405b41b947" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393395} {:answer/id #uuid "10025a8c-fe7f-5a1e-bd85-e7545c300aa3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393396}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69a990be-5c76-515e-a485-ec717c55c7a4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "k=-2,0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393394} :answer-field/id #uuid "74076d4c-7a3b-5be1-a5c4-009a1ee14ce3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186393391}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e2350a5d-3530-57c1-8b04-eddbcad053e9" :question/math-academy-id "q-74093" :question/problem "Let $a=[\\begin{aligned}k \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ and $b=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ k\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $a⊥b.$ Find the value of $k.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $a$ and $b$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that their dot product is equal to zero.\nFirst, let's calculate the dot product in terms of $k$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a\\cdot b & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {b}_{1}+{a}_{2}\\cdot {b}_{2}+{a}_{3}\\cdot {b}_{3}+{a}_{4}\\cdot {b}_{4} \\\\  & =k\\cdot 2+(-3)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot (-3)+k\\cdot k \\\\  & =2k+3-3+{k}^{2} \\\\  & =2k+{k}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating the dot product to zero, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2k+{k}^{2} & =0 \\\\ k(2+k) & =0 \\\\ k & =-2,0.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423655 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133771 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc757fa2-12ce-5a1c-a524-8eb256842079" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Dot Products Using Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ec71bf8-0520-59fa-9854-cb44ec045ef7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423632} {:answer/id #uuid "7a167fdb-738d-5b3d-8284-711c388657bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423633} {:answer/id #uuid "81c9e055-8cf2-5cc9-8653-4e0b2008470e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423635} {:answer/id #uuid "c98265c1-02be-53c4-83fe-a4d8b3bf3872" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-19" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423643} {:answer/id #uuid "fc670790-7eab-5b03-aa35-04d8fa5acbac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "77" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423646}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7a167fdb-738d-5b3d-8284-711c388657bf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "75" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423633} :answer-field/id #uuid "93222598-353b-55a9-b167-8027058d4fdc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423650}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c0dbf414-70a0-5f19-8395-cf58e2c89041" :question/math-academy-id "q-74158" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u\\,⊥\\,v.$ Find $(u-2w)\\cdot (w-4v).$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ and $v$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot v=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u-2w)\\cdot (w-4v) & =u\\cdot w-2(w\\cdot w)-4(u\\cdot v)+8(w\\cdot v) \\\\  & =u\\cdot w-2(w\\cdot w)+0+8(w\\cdot v) \\\\  & =(1\\cdot 3+4\\cdot (-2)+(-2)\\cdot 1+3\\cdot 2) \\\\  & -2(3\\cdot 3+(-2)\\cdot (-2)+1\\cdot 1+2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & +8(3\\cdot 2+(-2)\\cdot (-1)+1\\cdot 2+2\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =(3-8-2+6)-2(9+4+1+4)+8(6+2+2+4) \\\\  & =-1-36+112 \\\\  & =75.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186423652 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186133771 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "bc757fa2-12ce-5a1c-a524-8eb256842079" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating Dot Products Using Orthogonal Vectors"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0ab9e3e6-5284-58be-a5fe-0054619d52b2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423623} {:answer/id #uuid "2349f6f7-0b4a-5590-8339-7329d0015da4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423626} {:answer/id #uuid "41f8727d-5688-5fe2-ac9f-115c4c0396ab" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "37" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423630} {:answer/id #uuid "977bcee7-245e-56ff-a839-4b7982e8217d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "70" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423638} {:answer/id #uuid "a0f91a56-bedd-5b8c-8273-1ba24e48d009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "280" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423640}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a0f91a56-bedd-5b8c-8273-1ba24e48d009" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "280" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423640} :answer-field/id #uuid "53026106-93b7-5112-b460-a4866b2a53df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186423649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "4593be43-7c77-5707-9a35-2724546ec310" :question/math-academy-id "q-74183" :question/problem "Let $u=[\\begin{aligned}-5 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -3 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ and $w=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 8 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]$ be vectors such that $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal. Find $(u+2w)\\cdot (u-4v)-u\\cdot u.$" :question/worked-solution "Since $u$ and $w$ are orthogonal vectors, we have that $u\\cdot w=0.$ Therefore, using linearity of the dot product to expand the parentheses, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(u+2w)\\cdot (u-4v)-u\\cdot u & =u\\cdot u+(2w)\\cdot u-u\\cdot (4v)-(2w)\\cdot (4v)-u\\cdot u \\\\  & =2(w\\cdot u)-4(u\\cdot v)-8(w\\cdot v) \\\\  & =0-4(u\\cdot v)-8(w\\cdot v) \\\\  & =-4(u\\cdot v)-8(w\\cdot v) \\\\  & =-4((-5)\\cdot 1+(-3)\\cdot (-3)+2\\cdot (-4)+0\\cdot 1) \\\\  & -8(2\\cdot 1+2\\cdot (-3)+8\\cdot (-4)+3\\cdot 1) \\\\  & =-4(-5+9-8+0)-8(2-6-32+3) \\\\  & =16+264 \\\\  & =280.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
